    "description": "Product API has a 1,500 millisecond end-to-end latency objective. A page request fans out concurrently to Catalog, Pricing, Reviews, and Recommendation services. Each client currently has an independent 1,200 millisecond limit, and a request can also wait 300 milliseconds in the fan-out coordinator. If Pricing is slow, Product API continues running the other calls, and the server may complete them after the browser has disconnected. Catalog also forwards the request to Inventory with another fresh limit. The desired behavior is for one end-to-end time budget to remain authoritative across the whole call tree: each hop must limit outgoing work to the remaining time, pass the remaining budget and cancellation downstream, and avoid starting calls that cannot finish within it. Responses arriving after expiry must be discarded rather than included.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose how Product API should carry and enforce one end-to-end time budget across its concurrent downstream call tree so work stops when the original request can no longer succeed.",
    "notes": "Deadline-propagation fits carrying one end-to-end time budget with cancellation across the fan-out call tree; timeout and fail-fast are near-misses (tuned local bounds, refuse unfinishable work), supported by circuit-breaker for the slow dependency. Fixed family-term leakage by rewording timeout to limit and deadline to time budget."
  },
